A||N||8||DaRkLoRD||Headlight Modification Idea||||5||
Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||10-25-2000||06:24 PM||||Can you replace the bulbs in a daytime running light from say.. a Buick Skylark? (same one I measured the headlights from.. hehe..) I want to put in ultrabrights and use them as headlights.. (would this even work?)<P>then again, 2" tall headlights might be too short...??<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||lawrence||10-25-2000||07:04 PM||||The DRLs from a Buick are the major filament of a dual filament lamp.  In other words, they are just the turn signal filament on all the time.  They are not bright enough to be headlights, nor do they have the correct beam pattern.<P>Sorry,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||10-25-2000||08:02 PM||||im currently working on some permanent (non retracting) headlites for my car. I was going to use 93-96 Camaro there a little over 2" high-till I found out there $125.00 each and u need 4. Im now installing a set from an 87 Olds Toronado, there just about 3" high, $75 for the whole set including mounts. After theyre in im making clear lexan covers for them. There gonna be mounted all to body and Im trimming off the corners of hood around them, so when i open hood, lites will stay in fenders.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||10-25-2000||09:39 PM||||Lawrence, I'd like to make headlights similar to yours.. (in case you hadn't already guessed that. hehe) <P>I'm not looking forward to cutting up headlamps etc.. so I'm trying to find an existing headlight assembly that will fit. any car, as long as it works... Since the headlight door is 10" wide, I'm assuming I'll be able to use a headlight assembly that's 9" wide, with a half inch bezel on the edges. It would be mounted to the underside of the headlight doors. I'm not totally sure how I'll open and close them, since it's a different means of operation than the stock lights.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||lawrence||10-26-2000||12:34 PM||||If you are going to open the doors a different way, then yes, you can go as wide as the door (less bezel).  Otherwise, you need to stay inside the little cams that push the doors open.<P>How do you plan to open them?  You might be able to use the same concept as OEM, only lower/modify the motor frame, put a plate on the bottom of the headlamp fixtures, and push on that plate similarly to how it works now.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaRkLoRD||10-26-2000||12:52 PM||||I've been thinking of linear actuators or a stepper motor(s). a stepper motor would probably be easisest as they're fairly inexpensive (compared to actuators) and should be easy to find and wire up.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||SeanyD||10-26-2000||09:19 PM||||you could modify the front fascia to look like the ZX's. leaving a little bit of head light showing.....then wire them so you can turn them on without raising them....common thing with the 300's, uses about a 7x 2 " section of the headlight actually showing and its not supposed to affect it||||reg||1
Z||000007||SeanyD||10-26-2000||09:19 PM||||you could modify the front fascia to look like the ZX's. leaving a little bit of head light showing.....then wire them so you can turn them on without raising them....common thing with the 300's, uses about a 7x 2 " section of the headlight actually showing and its not supposed to affect it||||reg||1
